# Summary: Apache Doris is a high-performance, real-time analytical database
# based on the MPP architecture. Known for being efficient, simple, and unified,
# it returns query results over massive datasets within sub-second latency,
# and a single system supports both high-concurrency point queries and high
# throughput complex analytics.
#
# Desc: Apache Doris (Frontend) suffers from an arbitrary local file read through
# its JDBC catalog feature, and exposes an unsafe-deserialization option to the
# same attack surface. Connection properties for a JDBC catalog are passed to the
# MySQL driver without a safe allow-list (handleJdbcUrl() forces only benign params),
# so a user who can define or point a catalog at an attacker-controlled MySQL endpoint
# can enable dangerous driver options that the FE's JDBC client will honor. With
# allowLoadLocalInfile=true, a rogue MySQL server can answer any query with a LOCAL
# INFILE request and read arbitrary files from the FE host (fe/conf/fe.conf secrets,
# keytab, id_rsa).
#
# Additionally, because autoDeserialize is not forced off, a malicious server can
# return a crafted blob that the driver deserializes. This can lead to remote code
# execution only if a suitable gadget is present on the Doris classpath.
#
# Tested on: MySQL Connector/J 8.0.33
#            Doris FE 4.1.5
#            JDK 17
#
